{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/93452-ritual-sacrifices-and-the-afterlife-where-do-the-souls-of-sacrifice-victims-go","id":93452,"title":"Ritual sacrifices and the Afterlife.....where do the souls of sacrifice victims go?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-03-18T06:51:33-07:00","id":93452,"title":"Ritual sacrifices and the Afterlife.....where do the souls of sacrifice victims go?","content":"Okay, this has been rattling around in my head for a while. We had a brief talk about this on the old forum during my infamous Aztlanti sacrifice thread. (For those of you who remember it, let's NOT bring it up. For those of you who don't? Trust me, you're happier for it. Seriously, I was a moron on that thread.) But after reading some other stuff, I think there may be more room for discussion.<br /> <br /> Alright, so like the title says: many ancient cultures had the concept of human sacrifice. The Aztecs being the most (in)famous. In Scion's World, what do you think becomes of the souls of sacrifice victims after they die? Be they normal mortals, or even Scions and/or Gods.<br /> <br /> So, during that ill-fated thread, Brent Not Broken put forward the theory that, at least in the case of Aztec sacrifice, the Atzlanti don't really care about your soul. It's your BLOOD that they need for their rituals. Thus, it can be inferred that the souls of victims of Aztec sacrifice just go on to whatever Underworld they were destined for. A sacrificed Aztec would go to the Aztec Underworld, but a sacrificed Norse Scion would continue on to Valhalla.......or Helheim if the sacrifice was more of a straw death.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, it's a fairly common theory in Scion that death gods tend to be pretty greedy for souls, and geographical location can play a factor in which death god claims the soul of a recently dead person first. For example, if the aforementioned Norse Scion were sacrificed somewhere at least NEAR Northern Europe, odds are Hel and/or a Valkyrie would likely be able to claim his/her soul for the Aesir fairly easily. But, on the other hand, if the Norse Scion were sacrificed in Mexico, it's possible that Mictlantecuhtli could claim his/her soul instead.<br /> <br /> So, which do you think is more likely? If a Scion, or even a normal person, who is more resonant with another pantheon is killed in a ritual designed to honor a particular God and/or Pantheon, does he/she go to that Pantheon's Underworld, since he/she was killed in one of their rituals? Or do they go on to whatever Afterlife they were already heading for? Do sacrifice rituals send your soul to that Pantheon's Underworld as a matter of course? Or could geography be a factor?<br /> <br /> Also, in the case of sacrificed Scions and/or Gods, this may be a more important factor. Yeah, no one really cares if a random European mortal gets sent to Mictlan in a sacrifice, but Gods tend to be more possessive about important figures like their children. The aforementioned Norse Scion may be sent to Mictlan as part of the ritual and/or geographical point of death, but that could raise a political fuss if the Scion's divine parent cares enough to demand that his/her soul go to Valhalla. The Aesir especially may raise this issue as they need every soul they can get in their army once Ragnarok comes.<br /> <br /> Obviously, the Aztecs are the Pantheon we think of most in this line of thought, but we know that other cultures practiced human sacrifice at one time or another, so no need to limit discussion to only the Aztecs. For one thing, other cultures may have other ideas in their cosmology about where foreign souls killed in their rituals go. So let's keep the discussion open.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-18T09:35:07-07:00","id":93531,"title":"","content":"I tried to address this head-on, but I hadn't thought it through to the extent I guess I should have. <br /> <br /> I stated in the heading for my Death Purview that anyone killed by a Scion using it will go to the Underworld of said Scion. I guess there's an inference that anyone such a Scion kills will also go to said Underworld, but I'd probably adjudicate on a case-by-case basis (Legend disparity, experience with said Underworld, having the Death Purview). <br /> <br /> As for everyone else, that's my vague area. I'd have said that Scions go to their pantheon's Underworld. But the idea of land grabs, etc. to net souls is interesting. As is Bent's idea about the purpose of the death. If the purpose of Aztec sacrifice is the blood, then what? What's the purpose of a blood-eagle? It's not to get the soul to Valhalla, that's for sure...","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-18T09:43:32-07:00","id":93539,"title":"","content":"<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ajax</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/93452-ritual-sacrifices-and-the-afterlife-where-do-the-souls-of-sacrifice-victims-go?p=93531#post93531\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I tried to address this head-on, but I hadn't thought it through to the extent I guess I should have.<br /> <br /> I stated in the heading for my Death Purview that anyone killed by a Scion using it will go to the Underworld of said Scion. I guess there's an inference that anyone such a Scion kills will also go to said Underworld, but I'd probably adjudicate on a case-by-case basis (Legend disparity, experience with said Underworld, having the Death Purview).<br /> <br /> As for everyone else, that's my vague area. I'd have said that Scions go to their pantheon's Underworld. But the idea of land grabs, etc. to net souls is interesting. As is Bent's idea about the purpose of the death. If the purpose of Aztec sacrifice is the blood, then what? What's the purpose of a blood-eagle? It's not to get the soul to Valhalla, that's for sure...</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Hm. Your Death Purview idea sounds interesting. Do you have a link or something so I can check it out?<br /> <br /> I do think that the &quot;default setting&quot; is that Scion's go to their Pantheon's Underworld, but death gods CAN be kinda grabby about souls. You could easily see Mictlantecuhtli grabbing that Norse Scion's soul by saying &quot;Hey, his life was given to MY Pantheon. That means he belongs to me, now.&quot; It could also be petty vengeance: a Scion who offended a death god in some way may find their punishment as having to stay in that death god's realm forever.<br /> <br /> As for the blood eagle thing? That wasn't a human sacrifice, as far as I know. It was just a really cruel way to murder someone who pissed you off. I don't think it was considered any kind of holy rite. I also believe historians are still unsure if the blood eagle was ever even a real thing. At the very least, there isn't much to suggest that it was a very common practice.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-18T18:32:12-07:00","id":93992,"title":"","content":"Here's my thoughts on the matter...<br /> <br /> First of all, if someone gets sacrificed to an Aztec god, Mictlan is not an option. Similar to Helheim, Mictlan is the final resting place of those who die ignoble deaths. Those who die from water/rain related methods (drowning, lightning strikes, certain diseases like pneumonia or leprosy), as well as children, went to Tlalocan, Tlaloc's land of eternal springtime and plenty. That includes Tlaloc's sacrifices, who tended to be drowned children. Those who die on the sacrificial stone or in battle went with Huitzilopochtli to Tonatiuh-Ilhucac, a land of endless, glorious battle against the star monsters, ending with rest, parties, and trips back to the World as a hummingbird or monarch butterfly. <br /> <br /> For mortals, most gods probably aren't going to care very much at all about who's soul goes where. Under normal circumstances, I'd say that if a mortal is sacrificed by an Aztec god, they're most likely going to either Tlalocan or Tonatiuh-Ilhucac unless there's direct intercession by a deity or psychopomp of some kind, and that those intercessions are probably rare, and usually not contested by the Aztecs. <br /> <br /> If it's a Scion of a non-Aztec pantheon sacrificed, then the Scion's home pantheon almost assuredly gets dibs. The only possible time I could see this backfiring is if Huitzilopochtli wants an extremely good warrior to stand beside him and start laying waste to the stars. <br /> <br /> Then again, political ramifications for a Scion being sacrificed are going to be HUGE in ways beyond who gets claim to the soul, so I wouldn't stress it too much","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/267-griffinguy24","name":"griffinguy24","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar267_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-23T16:38:01-07:00","id":97121,"title":"","content":"This is not cannon, but I actually plays it once as any sacrificial victim lost their Fate, and so their soul entered the Underworld as a mortal who died having done nothing good or bad. Ad that the soul wen to the Underworld of Sacrificing entity. This could be a boon or a curse depending on the type of Underworld. I was inspired to do this, based upon the Greek idea of souls reincarnation and dying heroically three time before they received he BEST afterlife. Now being sacrificed meant your soul did not die heroically - ever.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1336-gideon","name":"Gideon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1336_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"84"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}